\section*{A deterministic linear walk clock on tree-weighted planar maps}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a proof in the selected manuscript. On tree-weighted planar maps, the stationary walk is claimed to converge to Brownian motion of a $\sqrt2$-Liouville sphere with exactly the edge count as its time factor.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Contour reconstruction is extended to constrained energy minima and extremal lengths. A primal--dual obstruction prevents annular energy degeneracy even without face-shape control; energy convergence and cutoff estimates then retain the conditional path law and determine the clock constant.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
On tree-weighted planar maps, the stationary walk is claimed to converge to Brownian motion of a $\sqrt2$-Liouville sphere with exactly the edge count as its time factor. The question retains these same parameters and conventions. Within these assumptions, this is a claimed affirmative answer to the stated question.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 211, the selected Lean theorem is the $q>4$ Brownian tree metric-measure limit, with degree measure and the $n^{-1/2}$ distance scale. The subcritical, critical-Liouville, walk, and spectral companions are not included. No Comparator is assigned to the selected manuscript here; the family page selects other papers or supporting statements. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

